export const sets = {
  "u4-ns1": passage("u4-ns1", "Firearms and warships in Asian seas",
    "Gunpowder weapons spread quickly along the sea routes of the 1500s. Portuguese ships carried cannons along their sides, and in 1509 a Portuguese fleet defeated a combined fleet sent by the sultan of Gujarat, the Mamluk sultan of Egypt, and the ruler of Calicut near Diu, off western India. The Ottomans answered by sending fleets and gunners into the Red Sea and the Indian Ocean, and in the 1560s they sent cannons and gunners to the sultanate of Aceh in Sumatra, a rival of the Portuguese. In 1543 a Chinese junk carrying Portuguese traders landed on the Japanese island of Tanegashima, and Japanese smiths soon copied the matchlock guns the traders brought. Within a few decades, Japanese warlords fielded thousands of musketeers, and at the battle of Nagashino in 1575 the forces of Oda Nobunaga used massed musket fire against charging cavalry. When Japanese armies invaded Korea in the 1590s, the Korean admiral Yi Sun-sin used covered, cannon-armed \"turtle ships\" to cut their supply lines by sea."),
  "u4-ns2": passage("u4-ns2", "Maps and the shape of the world",
    "World maps changed quickly as reports from voyages reached mapmakers and printers. In 1507 the German mapmaker Martin Waldseemüller printed a world map that showed the lands reached by Columbus and later Spanish and Portuguese captains as a separate continent, and he named it America after the Italian navigator Amerigo Vespucci. In 1513 the Ottoman admiral Piri Reis drew a world map on gazelle-skin parchment; in notes on the map he wrote that he had combined about twenty earlier charts, among them Arab, Portuguese, and Ptolemaic maps and one drawn by Columbus. Portuguese officials kept their best charts of the routes to Africa and Asia in Lisbon and tried to keep the details from rival states. In China, the Italian Jesuit Matteo Ricci worked with Chinese scholars to print a large world map in Chinese in 1602. It placed China near the center, and the Wanli emperor's court later had copies made."),
  "u4-ns3": passage("u4-ns3", "Why Portugal and Spain sailed",
    "Portugal's voyages began with conquest. In 1415 a Portuguese force captured Ceuta, a Muslim port in North Africa, and Prince Henry then paid for voyages down the West African coast that brought back gold and enslaved Africans and set up trading forts. Crusading ideals mattered too: the Portuguese hoped to find Christian allies against Muslim states and to win converts. Bartolomeu Dias rounded the southern tip of Africa in 1488, and Vasco da Gama reached Calicut in India in 1498, opening a sea route to the pepper and spices that Venetian and Muslim merchants had long carried to Europe. Spain's monarchs, Ferdinand and Isabella, backed Columbus in 1492, the same year they conquered Granada, the last Muslim kingdom in Iberia. In both kingdoms, monarchs who had strengthened their control over nobles and taxes could pay for voyages, and they expected a share of the profits."),
  "u4-ns4": passage("u4-ns4", "Northern Europeans look for other routes",
    "England, France, and the Dutch Republic entered transoceanic exploration later than Portugal and Spain, and their captains often looked for a northern passage to Asia that would avoid the routes their Iberian rivals controlled. John Cabot sailed for England's Henry VII in 1497 and reached Newfoundland. Jacques Cartier made three voyages for France between 1534 and 1542 and sailed up the St. Lawrence River, hoping it led to Asia. Henry Hudson searched for a passage first for the Dutch East India Company in 1609 and then for English merchants in 1610. None of them found a route to Asia. Their voyages, however, led to rich cod fisheries off Newfoundland and to a fur trade with Indigenous peoples, and in the early 1600s these states founded Jamestown (1607), Quebec (1608), and New Amsterdam (1620s)."),
  "u4-ns5": passage("u4-ns5", "American crops in Africa and Asia",
    "American food crops changed farming far from the Americas. Portuguese traders brought cassava (manioc) from Brazil to West Central Africa in the 1500s. Cassava grew in poor soils, survived drought, and could be left in the ground for months, and it became a staple in the Kongo and Angola regions, as maize did in parts of West Africa. In southern China, a merchant named Chen Zhenlong brought sweet potato vines from Spanish Manila to Fujian province in 1593, and officials there promoted the crop during a famine. Sweet potatoes and maize could be grown on hillsides unsuited to rice, and they helped feed the rapid population growth of the Qing period. Tobacco, another American plant, spread across Europe, Africa, and Asia within a century. Some rulers tried to stop its use; the Ottoman sultan Murad IV banned smoking in the 1630s, but other states soon taxed tobacco instead."),
  "u4-ns6": passage("u4-ns6", "Conquest and rule in Spanish America",
    "The Spanish conquests of the Mexica and Inca empires were quick, but not only because of Spanish weapons. Hernán Cortés's few hundred Spaniards fought beside tens of thousands of Indigenous allies, especially the Tlaxcalans, enemies of the Mexica, and a smallpox epidemic struck Tenochtitlan during the siege that ended in 1521. Francisco Pizarro captured the Inca ruler Atahualpa in 1532, just after a civil war between Atahualpa and his brother had divided the empire. The Spanish crown then built a large colonial government. Viceroys ruled New Spain (from 1535) and Peru (from 1542), royal courts called audiencias heard cases and checked local officials, and the Council of the Indies in Spain wrote laws for the colonies. The crown also limited the power of the conquistadors: the New Laws of 1542 banned the enslavement of Indigenous people and tried to end the inheritance of encomiendas. In 1565 Spain began the conquest of the Philippines, and in 1571 it made Manila the capital."),
  "u4-ns7": passage("u4-ns7", "Brazil and the sugar plantation",
    "Portuguese ships reached Brazil in 1500, but Portugal did little there until the 1530s, when the crown granted large coastal territories called captaincies to nobles who agreed to settle them at their own expense. Sugar soon became Brazil's main export. Planters in Pernambuco and Bahia built engenhos, sugar mills where cane had to be crushed and boiled within hours of cutting. They first forced Indigenous people to work, but epidemics, flight, and resistance reduced that labor, and planters turned to enslaved Africans bought through Portugal's trading forts in West and West Central Africa. By about 1600 Brazil was the world's largest producer of sugar. The Dutch West India Company seized Pernambuco in 1630 and held it until 1654. After the Portuguese drove the Dutch out, Dutch merchants and planters helped spread the techniques of sugar planting to English and French islands in the Caribbean, such as Barbados."),
  "u4-ns8": passage("u4-ns8", "The Dutch East India Company",
    "In 1602 the States General of the Dutch Republic chartered the United East India Company (Vereenigde Oostindische Compagnie, or VOC). Thousands of investors bought shares, and the company's capital stayed invested for years instead of being divided after each voyage; shares were bought and sold on the Amsterdam exchange. The charter gave the company a monopoly on Dutch trade east of the Cape of Good Hope and the right to build forts, keep soldiers, make treaties, and wage war. In 1619 its governor-general Jan Pieterszoon Coen made Batavia (Jakarta) on Java the company's headquarters. In 1621 Coen's forces conquered the Banda Islands, the only source of nutmeg; most of the islanders were killed, enslaved, or driven away, and the company replaced them with plantations worked by enslaved laborers. In 1641 the company took Malacca from the Portuguese."),
  "u4-ns9": passage("u4-ns9", "Challenges to rulers in the 1600s",
    "Rulers who expanded their power often faced revolts at home and resistance abroad. In France, nobles and the Parlement of Paris rebelled against royal taxes and ministers during the Fronde (1648 to 1653), while Louis XIV was a child. In Japan, peasants on the Shimabara peninsula and nearby islands, many of them Christians, rebelled in 1637 against heavy taxes and persecution; the Tokugawa shogunate crushed the revolt in 1638 and in 1639 expelled the Portuguese. In West Central Africa, Queen Njinga of Ndongo and Matamba fought Portuguese armies and their African allies for decades, made alliances with the Dutch when they held Luanda (1641 to 1648), and finally made peace with Portugal in 1656 on terms that kept her kingdom independent."),
  "u4-ns10": passage("u4-ns10", "Rank, service, and status",
    "Several states of this period tied a person's rank to service to the ruler. The Mughal emperor Akbar gave military and civil officials numbered ranks called mansabs, which set their pay and the number of cavalry they had to keep, and he paid many of them with the revenue of lands called jagirs, which were moved from place to place and were not supposed to pass to their heirs. In Russia, Peter the Great's Table of Ranks (1722) set fourteen grades of military, civil, and court service, and commoners who rose high enough became nobles. At the same time Russian serfs lost freedoms: a law code of 1649 bound them permanently to their masters' estates. In Tokugawa Japan, the shogunate ranked society by hereditary status, with samurai above farmers, artisans, and merchants, yet many merchants in cities such as Osaka grew richer than the samurai, who lived on fixed rice stipends and often borrowed from them."),
  "u4-ns11": passage("u4-ns11", "Silver, prices, and Spain's empire",
    "After the discovery of silver at Potosí in 1545 and at Zacatecas in Mexico in 1546, and after miners adopted a process using mercury to separate silver from ore, the Americas produced most of the world's new silver for two centuries. Much of it went to Spain, where the crown took a fifth as tax. Prices in Spain and then across Europe rose several times over during the 1500s, a change historians call the price revolution, and they link it partly to the flood of silver and partly to population growth. Silver did not make the Spanish crown secure. It spent heavily on wars in Europe and declared bankruptcy several times, starting in 1557. Large amounts of silver also left Europe and the Americas for Asia, especially China, where the Ming government had begun collecting taxes in silver. Japan, then the other great producer of silver, also sent much of its output to China."),
  "u4-ns12": passage("u4-ns12", "Old and new forms of coerced labor",
    "Coerced labor was old in 1450. Slavery existed in Africa, the Mediterranean, and the Islamic world, and serfdom bound peasants to estates in much of Europe. What changed after 1450 was the scale and shape of slavery in the Americas. Between 1500 and 1750 European ships carried several million enslaved Africans across the Atlantic, most of them to grow sugar and later other crops for export. In the Americas, slavery became hereditary, permanent, and closely tied to African descent. In the English colonies of the Chesapeake, tobacco planters at first relied mainly on indentured servants from England, who worked for four to seven years in return for their passage. In the later 1600s, as fewer English servants came and the supply of enslaved Africans grew, planters turned to slavery, and colonial laws made it a lifelong status passed from mother to child. In the Andes, Spanish officials adapted the Inca mit'a, a labor duty owed to the state, for their own mines."),
  "u4-ns13": passage("u4-ns13", "Land empires and sea empires, c. 1750",
    "In 1750 most people in Asia still lived under land-based empires much larger than the European holdings there. The Qing dynasty defeated the Zunghar Mongols in the 1750s and doubled the territory it ruled, adding Xinjiang to earlier gains in Mongolia and Tibet. Russia had pushed its control across Siberia to the Pacific, founding Okhotsk in 1647. The Ottoman Empire still ruled the Balkans, Anatolia, the Arab lands, and North Africa. European companies, by contrast, held mostly forts and ports in Asia, and they often traded on terms set by Asian rulers. Change came first where a land empire weakened. After the death of Aurangzeb in 1707, Mughal emperors lost control of many provinces to governors and regional powers, and the British East India Company and its French rival began fighting for influence among these new states."),
  "u4-ns14": passage("u4-ns14", "Religion in the early modern world",
    "Between 1450 and 1750, Christianity became a world religion through empire. Missionaries traveling with Spanish and Portuguese colonizers converted most of the Indigenous peoples under Spanish rule in the Americas and most lowland Filipinos, often blending Catholic practice with older beliefs. Where European states lacked power, missions had far less success. In Japan, Jesuits won perhaps 300,000 converts by about 1600, but the Tokugawa shogunate banned Christianity in 1614 and persecuted those who refused to give it up. In China, Jesuits served at the Ming and Qing courts as astronomers and mapmakers but made few converts. Meanwhile, Islam kept spreading in Southeast Asia, as it had before 1450, through Muslim merchants, Sufi teachers, and rulers who converted. Port states such as Aceh, Banten, and Makassar became centers of Islamic learning and trade."),
};

